 # Transformer-based Reconstruction
-
-
 ## Overview
-Transformer-based Reconstruction refers to a family of feed-forward neural network methods that directly predict 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) parameters from 2D image inputs, bypassing the iterative optimization required by standard 3DGS [G]. Unlike iterative approaches that refine a scene representation over time, these models infer the scene geometry and appearance in a single forward pass [G]. The family is distinguished from sibling rasterization and rendering optimizations by its focus on the generative reconstruction pipeline rather than the rendering speed or mathematical stability of the Gaussian primitives themselves [G].
-
+Transformer-based Reconstruction refers to a family of feed-forward neural network methods that directly predict 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) parameters from 2D image inputs, bypassing the iterative optimization required by standard 3DGS. Unlike iterative approaches that refine a scene representation over time, these models infer the scene geometry and appearance in a single forward pass. The family is distinguished from sibling rasterization and rendering optimizations by its focus on the generative reconstruction pipeline rather than the rendering speed or mathematical stability of the Gaussian primitives themselves.
 ## Shared principles
 All methods in this family share the following core mechanism:
-1. **Input Modality**: The input consists of 2D images (monocular or multi-view) and, for multi-view methods, corresponding camera parameters (intrinsics and extrinsics) [G].
-2. **Direct Parameter Prediction**: The network outputs the full set of 3D Gaussian parameters directly: mean position ($\mu$), covariance matrix ($\Sigma$), color ($c$), and opacity ($\alpha$) [G].
-3. **Transformer Architecture**: The core processing unit is a transformer block (or sequence of blocks) that attends to relationships between image features and camera poses to infer 3D structure [G].
-4. **Feed-Forward Nature**: The reconstruction is non-iterative; the output is generated in a single inference step without gradient descent or scene optimization loops [G].
-5. **Supervision**: Training is supervised using photometric losses (e.g., LPIPS, SSIM) comparing rendered outputs against ground-truth input images [G].
