 ## Background
-The concept of feed-forward reconstruction for 3D Gaussian Splatting emerged as a solution to the computational bottleneck of iterative optimization in standard 3DGS [G]. Traditional 3DGS requires thousands of optimization steps to converge, making it unsuitable for real-time applications from sparse inputs [G]. Transformer-based methods leverage the attention mechanism's ability to model long-range dependencies and spatial relationships, allowing them to infer 3D structure from 2D projections directly [G]. Flash3D pioneered the monocular approach by integrating depth estimation into the Gaussian prediction pipeline [Flash3D](1481-flash3d.md). GS-LRM extended this to sparse multi-view settings by explicitly incorporating camera parameters into the transformer's token sequence [GS-LRM](1483-gs-lrm.md). These methods represent a shift from optimization-based reconstruction to learning-based direct prediction [G].
